FIT_MORE_17 = [

('rpe-and-rir-explained', 'Understand', 'RPE and RIR, explained: the two numbers that replace a percentage', 'What RPE and reps-in-reserve actually measure, how to rate a set honestly, why the scales drift as you get experienced, and the errors that make them useless.', '<p>Most training programmes are written with a number you cannot look up: <b>leave two reps in reserve</b>, or <b>RPE 8</b>. Both mean the same thing, and both are estimates rather than measurements &mdash; which is exactly why they work as well as they do, and why they fail so predictably for beginners.</p>\n<h2>What the two scales are</h2>\n<p><b>RIR</b> is reps in reserve: how many more repetitions you could have completed with good technique before failure. Zero RIR is failure. Two RIR means you stopped with two honest reps left. <b>RPE</b> in a lifting context is rating of perceived exertion on a ten-point scale borrowed from the RIR system rather than the older heart-rate version: RPE 10 is failure, RPE 9 is one rep left, RPE 8 is two, and so on. The two scales are the same instrument with the labels moved, which is why programmes use them interchangeably.</p>\n<h2>Why programmes use them instead of percentages</h2>\n<p>A percentage of your one-rep max assumes your strength today is the same as the day you tested it. It is not: sleep, stress, illness, travel and cumulative fatigue all move it. RIR self-corrects, because it is rated on the set you just did rather than on a number from eight weeks ago. It also makes programmes work across lifters of different levels without recalculating anything, and it lets you push hard on the days you can and pull back on the days you cannot &mdash; which is the mechanism behind most sensible autoregulation.</p>\n<h2>The honest problem: beginners are bad at it</h2>\n<p>Estimating reps in reserve requires knowing what failure feels like, and most people new to lifting have never been near it. The research consistently finds that inexperienced lifters overestimate how many reps they have left &mdash; a set they call two RIR is often four or five. The fix is unglamorous: on a small number of sets, using a machine or a lift where failing is safe, actually take a set to failure and count what the last reps felt like. Do that a handful of times and the scale starts to mean something. Until then, treat your RPE ratings as an overestimate and progress by adding load when the top of your prescribed range feels genuinely hard.</p>\n<h2>Where RPE works well, and where it does not</h2>\n<p>It works well on compound lifts in moderate rep ranges where you can judge technique breakdown, and on isolation work where failure is safe. It is weak on explosive or technical lifts &mdash; a maximal clean or a heavy deadlift single ends for reasons that have little to do with muscular reserve &mdash; and on very high-rep sets, where cardiovascular and local burn blur the judgement. It is also weak when form itself is the limiting factor, because &ldquo;I could have done two more&rdquo; is not the same claim as &ldquo;I could have done two more well&rdquo;.</p>\n<h2>Using it in a real programme</h2>\n<p>Work in bands rather than single numbers: keep most working sets between two and four RIR, take the last set of an isolation exercise closer to one, and reserve true failure for the final set of a machine or dumbbell movement where bailing is easy. Progress by increasing load when you can hit the top of the rep range at your target RPE for every set. The relationship between these numbers and raw load is covered in <a href="/fitness/how-progressive-overload-works/">how progressive overload works</a>, and the rep ranges these ratings sit inside are in <a href="/fitness/how-many-reps-for-muscle/">how many reps for muscle</a>.</p>'),

('time-under-tension-explained', 'Understand', 'Time under tension, explained: what the research actually supports', 'The claim that slow reps build more muscle, the evidence behind it, the one place where slowing down genuinely helps, and why chasing seconds is the wrong target.', '<p>Time under tension is one of the most repeated ideas in lifting &mdash; the claim that a set should last a specific number of seconds, and that muscle grows in proportion to the time it spends working. The idea is not nonsense, but the version that circulates in programmes is more specific than the evidence supports.</p>\n<h2>What the claim actually says</h2>\n<p>TUT is the total time a muscle is loaded during a set: eight reps at a two-second lift and a three-second lowering is roughly forty seconds of tension. The popular prescription is that sets in a particular time band &mdash; commonly thirty to sixty seconds &mdash; are optimal for muscle growth, and that reps should therefore be performed slowly and deliberately to hit the window. The reasoning behind it is that mechanical tension and metabolic stress both rise with time, so longer sets must produce more growth.</p>\n<h2>What the evidence supports</h2>\n<p>When studies match the variables properly, the picture is less tidy than the prescription. Growth tracks reasonably well with the number of hard sets taken close to failure across a wide range of rep speeds and set durations, and rep tempo on its own does not reliably produce more hypertrophy when volume and effort are matched. Slowing a rep down does not add growth; what it usually does is reduce the load you can handle, which means fewer effective reps at the same effort. The honest summary: TUT is a <i>consequence</i> of doing enough hard work, not an independent lever you can turn.</p>\n<h2>The one place where slowing down genuinely helps</h2>\n<p>Tempo matters most where it improves the quality of the rep rather than its duration. Slowing the lowering phase makes a lift easier to control, reduces the temptation to use momentum, increases the range of motion you can actually load, and gives beginners the time to feel what the target muscle is doing. It also makes light weights feel hard, which is useful when equipment is limited or a joint is irritable. Those are real benefits &mdash; they are just benefits of technique and control rather than of accumulating seconds.</p>\n<h2>What to do with the idea in practice</h2>\n<p>Use a controlled tempo &mdash; roughly a one-to-two second lift and a two-to-three second lowering on most exercises, never a bounced or rushed rep &mdash; and then let the set length fall where it falls. Count hard sets and how close to failure they were, not seconds. If a programme prescribes a TUT window, read it as a proxy for &ldquo;do not rush and do not go to failure on every set&rdquo;, which is usually the actual intent. If you want a deliberate way to make a set harder without adding weight, the tempo lever is legitimate, and it is discussed alongside the other options in <a href="/fitness/progressive-overload-without-adding-weight-explained/">progressive overload without adding weight</a>.</p>'),

('tempo-training-explained', 'Train', 'Tempo training, explained: what 3010 means and when it is worth using', 'Reading a tempo prescription, what each digit controls, the specific problems tempo solves, and the four situations where it beats simply adding load.', '<p>A tempo written as <b>3010</b> looks like a code because it is one. Read correctly it is a precise instruction about the four phases of a repetition &mdash; and used deliberately it is one of the few ways to make a light weight do the job of a heavy one.</p>\n<h2>Reading the four digits</h2>\n<p>By convention the digits describe, in order: the <b>lowering</b> (eccentric) phase, the <b>pause</b> at the bottom, the <b>lifting</b> (concentric) phase, and the <b>pause</b> at the top. So 3010 means three seconds down, no pause, one second up, no pause at the top. A 2111 tempo means two down, one second paused at the bottom, one up, one at the top. Some programmes write the order differently, which is worth checking before you start &mdash; the convention is not universal, and the same four numbers in a different order is a different exercise.</p>\n<h2>What each phase is actually for</h2>\n<p>The <b>lowering phase</b> is where most of the useful work happens and where most people cheat by letting gravity do it. Slowing it down increases control, lets you load a fuller range of motion, and makes technique errors visible. The <b>pause</b> removes stored elastic energy and momentum, which means the muscles have to start the lift from a dead stop &mdash; the single most effective way to expose a weak point in a lift. The <b>lifting phase</b> is normally performed as hard and fast as control allows, even when the bar moves slowly, because the intention to move fast is itself a training stimulus. The <b>top pause</b> is mostly for exercises where lockout is the weak link or where a squeeze is the point.</p>\n<h2>The four situations where tempo beats more weight</h2>\n<p><b>First</b>, when you are working around an irritable joint: a slow, controlled tempo lets a lighter load produce a strong stimulus without the peak forces that aggravate it. <b>Second</b>, when equipment is limited &mdash; a pair of dumbbells and a tempo prescription is a genuinely effective home setup. <b>Third</b>, when technique is the limiting factor: pausing at the bottom of a squat or a press teaches position in a way that heavier loading cannot. <b>Fourth</b>, when you need to break a plateau without adding volume, because changing the tempo changes the demand on the same set and rep scheme.</p>\n<h2>What tempo will not do</h2>\n<p>It will not replace progressive overload. If the load, the reps and the effort stay constant for months, no tempo will keep you progressing. It also does not suit every exercise: slow eccentrics on Olympic-style lifts or heavy deadlifts add fatigue without adding much, and very long pauses can turn a strength exercise into something closer to a mobility drill. Used as a targeted tool on a small number of movements rather than a global prescription, it earns its place. The wider logic of what makes a set count is in <a href="/fitness/how-progressive-overload-works/">how progressive overload works</a>, and if you are new to loading at all, start with <a href="/fitness/strength-training-for-beginners/">strength training for beginners</a> before adding tempo prescriptions.</p>'),

('drop-sets-and-rest-pause-explained', 'Train', 'Drop sets and rest-pause: intensity techniques, honestly assessed', 'What drop sets, rest-pause, myo-reps and cluster sets actually do, the evidence on whether they beat normal sets, and the fatigue cost nobody mentions.', '<p>Intensity techniques are the part of training where marketing and evidence diverge furthest. They all do the same underlying thing &mdash; extend a set past the point where you would normally stop &mdash; and they all carry the same trade: more work per unit of time, at a higher recovery cost.</p>\n<h2>What each technique actually is</h2>\n<p>A <b>drop set</b> is a set taken close to failure, followed by an immediate reduction in load and more reps, sometimes repeated two or three times. <b>Rest-pause</b> is a set taken close to failure, followed by a very short rest of ten to thirty seconds, then more reps with the same load, repeated once or twice. <b>Myo-reps</b> is a structured version of the same idea: one long activation set, then several short clusters with brief rests. <b>Cluster sets</b> are the strength-training cousin &mdash; a set broken into small groups of reps with short rests between, which lets you move more total load at higher quality. Each one exists to squeeze additional reps out of a single working set.</p>\n<h2>What the evidence says</h2>\n<p>The consistent finding across comparisons is that these techniques produce broadly similar muscle and strength outcomes to conventional sets when total volume and effort are matched &mdash; and their real advantage is <b>time</b>. If you have thirty minutes and want the effect of an hour\'s worth of hard sets, a rest-pause or drop-set finisher is a legitimate way to compress it. Where they are marketed as superior per set, the evidence does not support that: the extra reps come at a cost in fatigue, and the cost has to be paid for somewhere.</p>\n<h2>The fatigue cost, which is the whole trade-off</h2>\n<p>Sets taken to or past failure generate disproportionate fatigue relative to the growth they add. That matters more for compound lifts than isolation ones, and more for someone training five or six days a week than someone training three. The practical rule that follows from it: use these techniques on the last set of an isolation exercise rather than on your main lift, keep them to one or two exercises per session, and do not run them every week. If your performance on the main lifts starts drifting down across sessions, that is the signal that the intensity work has taken over.</p>\n<h2>Which one to pick</h2>\n<p>Rest-pause is the most versatile and the easiest to dose, because the load stays the same and only the rest changes. Drop sets suit machines and dumbbells where reducing load takes seconds, and are awkward with a barbell. Myo-reps work well on small, safe isolation movements. Cluster sets are the least fatiguing relative to their benefit and belong in strength-focused training. For all of them, the question to ask is not &ldquo;is this advanced?&rdquo; but &ldquo;am I short of time or short of effort?&rdquo; &mdash; because intensity techniques solve the first problem and cannot solve the second. Recovery, not technique, is usually the limiting factor, which is why <a href="/fitness/rest-days-and-recovery/">rest days and recovery</a> matters more than any finisher, and why the soreness you feel after a drop set is worth reading properly in <a href="/fitness/muscle-soreness-vs-injury/">soreness versus injury</a>.</p>'),

('gym-acronyms-explained', 'Understand', 'Gym acronyms decoded: AMRAP, EMOM, RPE, RIR, 1RM and the rest', 'The abbreviations that make a workout plan unreadable, what each one means, where the definitions genuinely differ between gyms, and which ones you can ignore.', '<p>A workout written as &ldquo;EMOM 12: 3 power cleans @ RPE 8, then AMRAP 8 back squats&rdquo; is not complicated. It is just abbreviated, and the abbreviations are not standardised &mdash; which is why the same letters mean different things in a strength gym, a CrossFit box and a physiotherapy clinic.</p>\n<h2>The set-structure acronyms</h2>\n<p><b>AMRAP</b> has two distinct meanings, and context decides. In circuit and conditioning work it means &ldquo;as many rounds as possible&rdquo; in a time window. In strength work it means &ldquo;as many reps as possible&rdquo; on a single set, usually stopping short of total failure. <b>EMOM</b> is every minute on the minute: start a prescribed task at the top of each minute, and whatever time is left is your rest. <b>AFAP</b> is as fast as possible. <b>For time</b> means complete the work as quickly as you can. <b>Rounds</b> and <b>reps</b> are what they sound like, and <b>superset</b> means two exercises performed back to back with no rest between.</p>\n<h2>The effort and load acronyms</h2>\n<p><b>RPE</b> is rating of perceived exertion, on a ten-point lifting scale where ten is failure. <b>RIR</b> is reps in reserve, how many honest reps you had left &mdash; the same instrument as RPE with the numbers inverted, and both are covered properly in <a href="/fitness/rpe-and-rir-explained/">RPE and RIR explained</a>. <b>1RM</b> is your one-repetition maximum, the heaviest single you can lift; <b>5RM</b> is the heaviest five, and so on. Percentages in a programme are usually percentages of a tested or estimated 1RM. <b>TUT</b> is time under tension, and <b>ROM</b> is range of motion.</p>\n<h2>The programming acronyms</h2>\n<p><b>PR</b> and <b>PB</b> are personal record and personal best, and mean the same thing in different countries. <b>GPP</b> is general physical preparedness &mdash; the broad fitness base underneath sport-specific work &mdash; and <b>SPP</b> is specific physical preparedness. <b>WOD</b> is workout of the day, a class-format term. <b>Deload</b> is a planned lighter week, and <b>volume</b> in practice means total hard sets per week rather than anything to do with sound. <b>Eccentric</b> and <b>concentric</b> are the lowering and lifting phases of a rep, which is what tempo prescriptions control &mdash; see <a href="/fitness/tempo-training-explained/">tempo training explained</a>.</p>\n<h2>Which ones actually matter</h2>\n<p>Three carry real weight and the rest are vocabulary. <b>Reps in reserve</b>, because it determines whether you are training hard enough. <b>Volume</b>, as weekly hard sets per muscle group, because it is the main driver of adaptation and the main cause of overreaching. And <b>range of motion</b>, because a partial rep is a different exercise from a full one however it is written on the card. Everything else is shorthand for structure, and once you can read AMRAP and EMOM you can read almost any plan you will be handed.</p>\n<h2>The acronyms worth being suspicious of</h2>\n<p>Any abbreviation attached to a promise rather than a method. There is no acronym that changes what training does; there are only programmes with different structures and different recovery demands. If a plan\'s selling point is a proprietary initialism rather than a stated volume, intensity and progression, the initialism is the product.</p>'),

('progressive-overload-without-adding-weight-explained', 'Train', 'Progressive overload without adding weight: six levers that work', 'When you cannot add load — limited equipment, an irritable joint, a stalled lift — the six variables you can still progress, and the order to use them in.', '<p>Progressive overload gets explained as &ldquo;add weight every week&rdquo;, which is the one version of it that stops working. Overload means increasing the demand on the body over time, and load is only one of several ways to do that &mdash; often not even the best one. Knowing the other levers is what keeps training working when the bar cannot go up.</p>\n<h2>Why load eventually stops being available</h2>\n<p>Three situations account for most of it. <b>Equipment limits</b> &mdash; a home setup with fixed dumbbells, or a gym where the machine tops out. <b>Physical limits</b> &mdash; a joint that objects to heavy loading, a return from time off, or an age-related shift in what the body tolerates. And <b>progression limits</b> &mdash; after the first year or two, adding load weekly is simply not available to anyone who is not a beginner. The base mechanism is the same one described in <a href="/fitness/how-progressive-overload-works/">how progressive overload works</a>; what follows is what to do when the obvious lever is blocked.</p>\n<h2>Lever one: more reps at the same load</h2>\n<p>The simplest and most reliable. If you did three sets of eight last week at the same weight, three sets of ten this week is more work, and it is a genuine overload. Use a rep range rather than a fixed number &mdash; say eight to twelve &mdash; and only increase the load when you can hit the top of the range on every set at your target effort. This alone carries most people through their first years of training.</p>\n<h2>Lever two: more sets</h2>\n<p>Volume is the main driver of adaptation, and going from three working sets to four is a larger change than most people assume. It is also the lever most likely to be overused: adding sets raises fatigue faster than anything else, so treat it as a temporary tool for a training block rather than a permanent direction, and pull it back down before performance on your main lifts starts to fall.</p>\n<h2>Lever three: range of motion</h2>\n<p>A deeper squat, a fuller press, a straighter-arm pull-up is a harder repetition at the same load. This is the most underrated lever available, because it improves the exercise and the overload simultaneously. Deficit work, elevated feet, a slower bottom position and eliminating the bounce out of a rep all belong here.</p>\n<h2>Lever four: tempo and pauses</h2>\n<p>Slowing the lowering phase and pausing at the hardest point removes momentum and elastic rebound, which means the muscles do work they were previously getting help with. A paused squat at eighty kilos is a meaningfully different stimulus from an unpaused one at the same weight. The mechanics are covered in <a href="/fitness/tempo-training-explained/">tempo training explained</a>.</p>\n<h2>Lever five: less rest, and more density</h2>\n<p>The same total work completed in less time is more demanding on the systems supplying it. Cutting rest from three minutes to two between hard sets raises the cost of every set without touching the load. Rest-pause and drop-set structures are the extreme end of this lever &mdash; see <a href="/fitness/drop-sets-and-rest-pause-explained/">drop sets and rest-pause</a> &mdash; and they are best kept to isolation work.</p>\n<h2>Lever six: better technique at the same everything</h2>\n<p>The least exciting and most durable. Removing a compensation, bracing properly, controlling the bar path and finishing the range all increase the load the target tissue actually receives. It does not feel like progress because nothing on the card changes, and it is the version of overload that still works twenty years in. Progress here is best judged by whether the same weight now feels controlled rather than survived &mdash; and if a set leaves you unsure whether something is working or warning, <a href="/fitness/muscle-soreness-vs-injury/">soreness versus injury</a> is the read you need first.</p>'),

]

FIT_SOURCES_17 = {'rpe-and-rir-explained': [('PubMed — rating of perceived exertion and resistance training load', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/'), ('ACE — training intensity and autoregulation guidance', 'https://www.acefitness.org/')], 'time-under-tension-explained': [('PubMed — repetition duration and hypertrophy research', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/'), ('Wikipedia — Weight training', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weight_training')], 'tempo-training-explained': [('ExRx — exercise tempo and lifting phases', 'https://exrx.net/'), ('PubMed — tempo and repetition-speed studies', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/')], 'drop-sets-and-rest-pause-explained': [('PubMed — advanced resistance-training techniques and hypertrophy', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/'), ('Wikipedia — Drop set', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drop_set')], 'gym-acronyms-explained': [('ACE — exercise and programming terminology', 'https://www.acefitness.org/'), ('ExRx — weight training glossary', 'https://exrx.net/')], 'progressive-overload-without-adding-weight-explained': [('Wikipedia — Progressive overload', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Progressive_overload'), ('PubMed — resistance training volume and hypertrophy', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/')]}
